Date: 20 Jan 94 02:31:44 EST
From: "Scott C. Herman" <70400.1036@compuserve.com>
Subject: The singularity: the "so-so" future

NOTE:   I am borrowing Scott Herman's CompuServe account to post
messages to the List, so please address your responses to me,
Mark Plus.

	To reiterate Julian L. Simon's "sufficiently well"
principle:

>The crucial contributions to living that advances in productive
>technique might make in the long-run future differ fundamentally
>from those that it has made in the past and immediate future. 
>We now possess knowledge about resource locations and materials
>processing that allows us to satisfy our physical needs and
>desires for food, drink, heat, light, clothing, longevity
>[again, Julian is wrong about this one -- MP], transportation,
>and the recording and transmission of information and
>entertainment.  *We can perform these tasks sufficiently well so
>that additional knowledge on these subjects will not
>revolutionize life on earth.* [my emphasis -- MP]  
(from "Paradoxes and difficulties in the evaluation of progress
and technological advance: past and future," TECHNOLOGY IN
SOCIETY, Vol. 10, pp. 425-32 [1988])

	Perhaps many Extropians will hereafter view Dr. Simon as
"Julian the Apostate," but I think his position is quite
defensible.  From his analysis I infer that in a great many
areas, further technological progress won't make that much
difference in our lives, suggesting that "advances in productive
technique" as a source of economic growth is becoming exhausted. 
Unfortunately, I fear a great many Extropians will continue to
confuse convenience with necessity, and be fascinated by
comparatively trivial advances which won't significantly alter
our lives.  
	For a really fundamental example, consider electricity.  The
real cost of electricity has declined by about 90% (an order of
magnitude!) in this century.  Whittling away at the remaining 10%
won't make that much economic difference.  Specifically, about a
third of the current cost of electricity is fuel.  If fuel were
to become free all of a sudden (which is approximately the case
in Norway, with its abundant hydroelectric resources), our
electric bills would become at most a third cheaper.  While such
a saving would be nice, it would not revolutionize our standard
of living.  (Upon request I can supply a real-life example from
the motel I manage.)
	As I promised in earlier postings, now I will discuss
productivity and our transition over to the knowledge-and-
services economy.  Labor productivity is usually defined as
"simply the output divided by the number of workers or, more
often, by the number of hours worked" (refer to Sylvia Nasar's
article on "Productivity" in THE FORTUNE ENCYCLOPEDIA OF
ECONOMICS, pp. 222-26).  In our century U.S. productivity growth
averaged about 2% a year up to the Second World War, then from
the war until 1973 about 3% a year.  A 3% growth rate means that
productivity was doubling every 23 years.  Writing in their 1967
book THE YEAR 2000, futurists Herman Kahn and Anthony Wiener
argued that U.S. productivity would continue to grow in the 3-4%
range through the end of the century.  
	Since 1973, however, U.S. productivity has grown a feeble 1%
a year, giving a doubling time of 70 years.  Real wages have
continued to lag as well.  The tax reforms of the Reaganarchy
were motivated in part by the desire to get productivity growing
again, but to no effect.  Most of the seeming "prosperity" of the
1980's was the result of more production facilitated by foreign
investment and the entry of women and immigrants into the work
force.  But overall productivity has continued to stagnate.  Some
analysts argue that real wages have also changed very little over
the last 20 years, if they haven't declined.  Americans on
average today live little better than they did back when "The
Brady Bunch" was a new series.  Similarly, the saving rate has
continued to decline, despite the Reaganarchy's pro-saving
policies.
	Economists are frankly baffled by this 20-year-long
stagnation in productivity.  It has persisted through the rise
and collapse of oil prices, several business cycles, and radical
ideological shifts in Administrations.  I argue that the causes
are, in fact, two-fold.
	The first is the exhaustion of the sources of economic
progress.  Advances in productive technique, market expansion,
and capital accumulation have accomplished most of what they can
terrestrially accomplish.  The order-of-magnitude improvements in
our standard of living are already behind us; future improvements
will be on the order of a few percent.
	The second cause is our transition from a manufacturing
economy over to a knowledge-and-services economy.  In this
analysis I am indebted to Peter F. Drucker, whose excellent book
POST-CAPITALIST SOCIETY has helped me to understand our economic
predicament, though I disagree with some of his conclusions.  
	First, a disclaimer:  Drucker argues that our society is
"post-capitalist" not because it is becoming socialist (though
that is plausibly the case), but rather because the big
concentrations of capital these days belong not to individual
capitalists so much as to pension funds.  Because pension funds,
which invest heavily into the equity and bond markets, are
trustees of the retirement wealth of millions of Americans, it
can be argued that through a capitalist mechanism we are
approaching the socialist ideal of common ownership of the means
of production.  But that's neither here nor there.
	The important passages of Drucker's book are pp. 32-40,
where Drucker explains the history of productivity, and pp. 83-
96, where he discusses the productivity problems of knowledge
workers and service workers.  Drucker argues that our revolution
in lifestyles really took off about 100 years ago when Frederick
W. Taylor began the scientific study of work.  By analyzing sand-
shoveling and other tasks, Taylor reorganized them into greater
efficiency and started the revolution in manufacturing
productivity.  Since factory workers began to generate an
exponentially growing pool wealth from their input, they were
able to earn exponentially growing wages.  In the last 100 years
productivity has increased by about a factor of 50 in the
developed countries.  The improvements in our standard of living
defused the class struggle predicted by Marxists and feared by
conservatives in the latter 19th century.
	However, this productivity revolution has failed by its
success.  With growing efficiency in making and moving things,
fewer and fewer workers are required in manufacturing.  A few
decades ago manufacturing workers were the majority in the
workforce of the U.S. and other developed countries.  Today in
the U.S. less than 15% of the workforce works in manufacturing. 
Farm workers account for perhaps another 4% or less (they being
the other productivity success story), so the remaining 80+
percent of us are perforce knowledge workers or service workers. 
Even when manufacturing and farming workers become more
productive, their input into overall labor productivity has
become less and less important.  Drucker warns on p. 82 that the
productivity of knowledge workers and service workers, "rather
than the productivity of the people who make and move things, is
_the_ productivity of a developed economy.  It is abysmally low. 
The productivity of people doing knowledge work and service work
may actually be going down rather than going up."   
	Even more remarkable, on p. 83 Drucker warns that "Unless we
can learn how to increase the productivity of knowledge workers
and service workers, and increase it fast, the developed
countries will face economic stagnation and severe social
tension.  People can only get paid in accordance with their
productivity.  Their productivity creates the pool of wealth from
which wages and salaries are then paid.  If productivity does not
go up, let alone it it declines, higher _real_ incomes cannot be
paid."  Drucker then devotes the rest of the chapter to a
discussion of organizational models and examples which he thinks
will solve this problem.
	Here is where I part company with Drucker.  I suppose the
kinds of knowledge work involving data processing could be made
more productive, but the productivity of service work, which
employs a greater percentage of the workforce, faces (a) human
motor limits, and (b) limits from the nature of human social
interaction.  
	Regarding (a), Drucker describes a company, "outsourced" by
a hospital to do cleaning, which has learned how to change the
sheets on a bed in a third of the time it used to take.  I manage
a motel, so I find this plausible; some people clean rooms much
faster than others.  But no matter how much experience this
company accumulates, it will never learn how to change a bed in a
*fiftieth* of the former time; it would have to do so to match
the revolution in manufacturing productivity.  So this kind of
service productivity is facing some pretty hard contraints.
	And regarding (b), a great deal of service work, as in
department stores and restaurants, involves social interaction; 
this can't be time-and-motioned studied into greater productivity
without alienating the public.  Retail clerks and waitresses now
outnumber manufacturing workers in the U.S.  The productivity of
the former two categories of workers hasn't gone up in decades,
and probably won't go up in decades to come.  It's ironic that
television shows people shopping in malls as a sign of a nation's
"affluence."  Retail clerks are among the *least* productive
workers in the modern economy, as reflected by their low wages
(of which I know from direct experience).  The jokes on "Married
With Children" about Al Bundy's dead-end career as a shoe
salesman are funny precisely because of this reality.
	So in addition to retail clerks and waitresses, if you add
in barbers, teachers, taxi drivers, mechanics, etc., you have a
growing mass of workers with essentially fixed productivity. 
Given these facts, I don't see how we are supposed to get around
Drucker's predicted economic stagnation.  After climbing out of
the preindustrial economic valley over the last 200 years, we
find ourselves on a postindustrial economic plateau stretching
all the way to the horizon.  We're facing the Sub-Optimal
Spontaneous Order, or  "So-So" Future.         	    
