  I don't care how much standard economic analysis you apply, I disagree
with your point on basic principle. My standard of living is directly
related to the amount of "stuff" that can be produced per capita. The more
stuff out there, the cheaper it is, the higher my standard of living.
Regardless of my individual productivity, I will benefit. To claim that
productivity gains are finished, is in my opinion, unsupportable.
Forget about human manufacturing productivity. It doesn't matter if everyone
worked at McDonalds -- if every factory in this country was automated, the
standard of living for those people would go up. Gains in manufacturing
productivity are not over. Today's Manufacturing is still a wasteful,
bulky, laborious task. If goods get so cheap, they will be essentially
free or "post-scarce". Then it won't matter how productive you are or where
you work.

  Consider Hans Moravec's proposed society where no one works in manufacturing
and all goods are produced in advanced (perhaps nanotech/bush robot) factories
which pay social security taxes for the rest of society. Such people
would obviously have a higher standard of living even if their personal
productivity was very low.
 
  You continue to ignore economic gains by technological developments.
The costs of producing the average good is nowhere near the cost
of the energy and materials involved in its production, meanwhile billions
of people in other countries want that good but can't afford it. Tremendous
potential for expansion. If the two most speculative areas of research,
AI and Nanotech, pay off in even small amounts, the gains in productive
technique will make your predictions sound like the predictions
of flying cars and underwater cities we are supposed to be surrounded
by now.

  I agree that employment in the future will be primarly in knowledge
and services. However, applying the standard definition of productivity
to things like software doesn't work at all. How you measure the
"output" of a programmer? # of lines of code? # of applications written?
Quality of code ? Number of copies produced (virtually infinite). What
about entertainment? If two people each produce one movie/year, that isn't
a good measure of their productivity at all. What about the length of
the movie? The quality of the FX, the acting? 

  As for the "neccessary or convenient" argument, I ask you,
was the FAX machine really neccessary? In hindsight, you might say yes,
but how do you know what is "neccessary or convenient" when such
expectations change with our culture. Argument from lack of imagination?
And whether or not something is neccessary or not has no bearing on its
impact on our lives. We are a culture of conveinience. Going from 9600
to 14,400 baud was conveinient but it made a big difference in an
activity I do daily. Getting a 1.544 megabit connection will be a revolution
for me personally. Marginal gains in technology are enough to please
most people. Slightly faster, slightly smaller, slightly cheaper is
good enough for most people.


 Ok, there isn't going to be any invention or product as revolutionary
as the invention of electricity, the computer, or the steam engine. All
I can say is "so what?" Technological progress will continue to make
a big difference in our lives, especially within healthcare and biotech, and
this is your most indefensible point. (I know people for whom the invention
of the Newton was a tremendous advancement)


