Date: Thu, 20 Jan 1994 01:13:14 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The singularity: the "so-so" future

I wish to strongly support Mark Plus's pessimistic projection. He is
right that productivity has stopped increasing and that we face a
bleak future.

He cites electricity costs, noting that Norwegians have essentially
very cheap electricity, and yet their standard of living has not
zoomed upward. (Skeptics might say that this is because electricity
costs are only a small fraction of the input into the standard of
living equation, but we can ignore this for the sake of proving Mark's
point.)

An even better example is found in looking through past issues of "The
Equestrian Monthly," sister publication of "Fortune." Under "buggy
whips," essential for driving horses, we find the following:

* Cost in 1910: $2.73

* Cost in 1990: $14.31

Thus we see that our so-called manufacturing expertise is actually
resulting in _more expensive_ buggy whips, thus proving that our
standard of living is _declining_. In fact, analysis of "cost of
living" records suggests that 1910 was in fact the high point of our
history.

(I hear you skeptics pointing to the fact that in 1910 there were no
computers, no jet aircraft, primitive medicine, a longer work week,
fewer books, and so on. Well, these things are left out of "living
standard" calculatons for a good reason: they confuse the business
economists, who like to think in terms of things they already know.
Plus, it's good experimental technique to have "controls"...if VCRs
and cellular phones didn't exist in 1970, or 1950, or 1910, then how
can they be taken into account *now* when calculating standard of
living, cost of living, and overall productivity? Counting in all
these new gadgets and medical procedures would mess up the
calculations!)

I am indebted to Mark Aristos Plus, a True Extropian, for showing me
the way with the logic of his arguments!

Give up, folks. The best years are behind us, way behind us.


--Klaus! von Past Prime

