Transubstantiation" and "Primeval Revival"?  (The later sounds like you're
confusing me with McKenna's "Archaic Revival".)  If these terms are 
supposed to be funny, I'm not amused.  I tried hard to avoid religious
attitudes and assertions in the Principles, and I believe I succeeded.  There
is nothing like transubstantiation.  Despite the popularity of uploading among
Extropians, I do not give it any special position, do not assert it will 
happen and (in my Self-Tranformation essay in EXTROPY #10), explicitly warned
against treating the idea in a religious manner as some kind of Rapture.  I
resent your casting my writing in religious terms, and would appreciate a 
retraction.  If anyone is treating some Extropian ideas in a religious fashion,
it's not me.

Tim May also says:
< I think there's a very real danger that this list is evolving into a
< kind of support group/"Lifespring"-type movement that is, in many 
< ways, equivalent to a religion.  While the trappings of God and
< prophets are seemingly gone, in fact there is a set of Holy Books -- the
< back issues of "Extropy" [...], the same appeals to authority (Principle
< Five says..."), and the same pseudo-religious cant about the nature of the
< Universe and Man's place in it.

Tim makes extreme statements in his usual inflammatory style.  He reacts to
Kennita's postings by claiming the list and the whole Extropian movement is 
in danger if becoming a religious, Lifespring-like group.  Rubbish.  While
some of Kennita's postings are not to my taste, her often emotional writing
hardly makes the entire list or the movement as a whole into an encounter 
or therapy group.

Stating that the back issues of Extropy are Holy Books is more irresponsible 
and irritating nonsense.  I have never promoted them as such, and I don't 
recall seeing anyone treat them as such.  Extropy is the core of the movement,
having given rise to this list (thanks to Perry), and its lengthy articles 
naturally serve as useful references in many discussions.  Something does not
become a Holy Book simply by being referred to (as I believe Ray noted).
BTW, some time ago, Tim stated that he believed that very few, if any
List members found their way here because of the journal.  Another irritating
statement (that I *know* to be false) made without any support whatsoever.
The influx to the list should increase now that EXTROPY is featuring
selections from List discussions. 

Nor is talk about the nature of the universe (not that I've said much about 
that) and "Man's" place in it [I prefer not to use the term "Man" for humans]
"pseudo-religious cant".  Unless, that is, all philosophical reflection
about life is thereby religion.  Either Tim is abusing the word religion, or
else really doesn't care to present Extropianism fairly.

Tim again:
< The Principles are already creating a dogma.  People attempt to win their
< arguments by quoting from the Sacred Principles.

Wrong again!  The Principles are doing no such thing.  They very deliberately 
and clearly embody the ideas of continual improvement and revision (unlike
dogmatic texts they even have version numbers, for reason's sake!), individual
and critical thinking.  If some people try to treat them as sacred dogma,
the fault lies with them.  Such people are fools and are certainly failing
to live in an extropian manner to the extent that they have faith.  However,
unlike Tim, I see little evidence that people are doing this.  Often comparison
to the Principles is a shorthand way of evaluating a proposal according to 
already thought-out and accepted values.  The important thing is to be aware
of why (and to what extent) one agrees with the Principles, so that criticism 
of an action or proposal by saying "that goes against the Principles" can be 
expanded into a full explanation and defense as necessary.

Tim May also worries that "Extropianism is taking itself a bit too seriously,
and is adopting its own rituals and dogmas".  Enough about dogmas; but what's 
wrong with rituals?  Every group which shares ideas or activities has rituals 
in an innocuous sense.  Rituals serve an important function and are quite 
harmless so long as you remember that they are not the only way things can be
done and are not *required* by divine law to be done a particular way.  Tim:
Do you refuse to ever shake hands on the ground that it's a ritual?

Tim finds that the Extropian Principles haven't affected his life.  Well
fine for him.  However, they *have* affected other people and in a beneficial
way, I'm told. You may have gained nothing from the Principles (not surprising
given that you apparently have not understood them), but that's not a good 
reason to smear them and me with statements about "platitudes", "dogma",
"religion", and "Holy Books".  Nor do I appreciate being referred to as
"The Mother Church in Riverside".  I've become weary of insult poorly
disguised as humor.


